===Verb===
{{en-verb|rides|riding|rode|ridden|past2=rade|past2_qual=obsolete|past3=rid|past3_qual=obsolete|past_ptc2=rode|past_ptc2_qual=now colloquial and nonstandard}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|transitive}} To [[transport]] oneself by sitting on and directing a [[horse]], later also a [[bicycle]] etc. {{defdate|from 8th c., transitive usage from 9th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''ride''' to work every day and park the bike outside the office.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1|passage=Go Peto, to horse: for thou, and I, / Haue thirtie miles to '''ride''' yet ere dinner time.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Mansfield Park|chapter=XV|volume=I|page=310|passage={{...}} I will take my horse early to-morrow morning and '''ride''' over to Stoke, and settle with one of them.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Henry Esmond|volume=II|chapter=I Go on the Vigo Bay Expedition, Taste Salt Water and Smell Powder|page=96|passage=He '''rid''' to the end of the village, where he alighted and ſent a man thence to Mr. Tuſher with a meſſage that a gentleman of London would ſpeak to him on urgent buſineſs.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Mrs. Rinehart|journal=Time|date=28 Apr 1923
|passage=It is characteristic of her that she hates trains, that she arrives from a rail-road journey a nervous wreck; but that she can '''ride''' a horse steadily for weeks through the most dangerous western passes.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The Guardian|date=6 Oct 2010|titleurl=http://www.guardian.co.uk/sport/2010/oct/06/commonwealth-games-gymnastics-gold-silver-england
|passage=The original winner Azizulhasni Awang of Malaysia was relegated after '''riding''' too aggressively to storm from fourth to first on the final bend.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|transitive}} To be transported in a vehicle; to travel as a passenger. {{defdate|from 9th c., transitive usage from 19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|text=Now, in calm weather, to swim in the open ocean is as easy to the practised swimmer as to '''ride''' in a spring-carriage ashore.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Biznelcmd|journal=Time|date=20 Jun 1960
|passage=In an elaborately built, indoor San Francisco, passengers '''ride''' cable cars through quiet, hilly streets.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal|chiefly [[US]] and [[w:South Africa|South Africa]]}} To [[transport]] (someone) in a vehicle. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The cab '''rode''' him downtown.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of a ship: to [[sail]], to float on the water. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Ovid Art of Love|pages=19–20|pageref=20|passage=Why name I ev'ry Place where Youths abound? / 'Tis Loſs of Time; and a too fruitful Ground. / The ''Bajan'' Baths, where Ships at Anchor '''ride''', / And wholeſome Streams from Sulphur Fountains glide: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Crusoe|passage=By noon the sea went very high indeed, and our ship '''rode''' forecastle in, shipped several seas, and we thought once or twice our anchor had come home {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To be carried or supported by something lightly and quickly; to travel in such a way, as though on horseback. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The witch cackled and '''rode''' away on her broomstick.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To traverse by riding.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=David Levinson|author2=Karen Christensen|title=Encyclopedia of World Sport: From Ancient Times to the Present
|passage=Early women tobogganists '''rode''' the course in the requisite attire of their day: skirts. In spite of this hindrance, some women riders turned in very respectable performances.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To convey, as by riding; to make or do by riding.
#: {{ux|en|How many races have you '''ridden''' this year?}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Marmion|passage=The only men that safe can '''ride''' / Mine errands on the Scottish side.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To support a rider, as a horse; to move under the saddle.
#: {{ux|en|A horse '''rides''' easy or hard, slow or fast.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|transitive|slang|vulgar}} To [[mount]] (someone) to [[have sex]] with them; to have [[sexual intercourse]] with. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1997|author=Linda Howard|title=Son of the Morning|page=345
|passage=She '''rode''' him hard, and he squeezed her breasts, and she came again.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|colloquial}} To [[nag]] or [[criticize]]; to [[annoy]] (someone). {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2002|author=Myra MacPherson|title=Long Time Passing: Vietnam and the haunted generation|page=375
|passage=“One old boy started '''riding''' me about not having gone to Vietnam; I just spit my coffee at him, and he backed off.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of clothing: to gradually [[move]] (up) and [[crease]]; to [[ruckle]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ann Kessel|journal=The Guardian|date=27 Jul 2008|titleurl=http://www.guardian.co.uk/sport/2008/jul/27/3
|passage=In athletics, triple jumper Ashia Hansen advises a thong for training because, while knickers '''ride''' up, ‘thongs have nowhere left to go’: but in Beijing Britain's best are likely, she says, to forgo knickers altogether, preferring to go commando for their country under their GB kit.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[rely]], [[depend]] (on). {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Grappling with deficits|journal=The Economist|date=9 Mar 2006
|passage=With so much '''riding''' on the new payments system, it was thus a grave embarrassment to the government when the tariff for 2006-07 had to be withdrawn for amendments towards the end of February.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of clothing: to [[rest]] (in a given way on a part of the body). {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jenny Eliscu|title=Oops...she's doing it again|journal=The Observer|date=16 Sep 2001|titleurl=http://www.guardian.co.uk/theobserver/2001/sep/16/life1.lifemagazine6
|passage=She's wearing inky-blue jeans that '''ride''' low enough on her hips that her aquamarine thong peeks out teasingly at the back.}}
# {{lb|en|lacrosse}} To play [[defense]] on the defensemen or midfielders, as an attackman.
# To manage [[insolently]] at will; to [[domineer]] [[over]].
#* {{RQ:Swift The Presbyterians Plea of Merit}}
#*: The nobility{{...}} could no longer endure to be '''ridden''' by bakers, coblers[sic], brewers, and the like.
# {{lb|en|surgery}} To overlap (each other); said of bones or fractured fragments.
# {{lb|en|radio|television|transitive}} To [[monitor]] (some component of an audiovisual signal) in order to keep it within acceptable bounds.
#: {{co|en|vocal '''riding'''}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|author=Simran Kohli|title=Radio Jockey Handbook
|passage=The board operator normally watches the meter scale marked for modulation percentage, '''riding''' the gain to bring volume peaks into the 85% to 100% range.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2017|author=Michael O'Connell|title=Turn Up the Volume: A Down and Dirty Guide to Podcasting|page=22
|passage=“You don't want them '''riding''' the volume knob, so that's why you learn how to do your levels properly to make the whole thing transparent for the listener. {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|music}} In [[jazz]], to play in a steady rhythmical style.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2000|author=Max Harrison|author2=Charles Fox|author3=Eric Thacker|title=The Essential Jazz Records: Modernism to postmodernism|page=238
|passage=The quintet in Propheticape muses out-of-measured-time until Holland leads it into swift, '''riding''' jazz.}}

